  "shared": {
    "translations": {
      "en": {
        "title": "How code runs",
        "story": "A desk sign can contain two lines. This program has two output statements, and each prints one message. Before running it, put the messages in the order you expect to see them. Use a new line between the two words on your guess slip. The runtime carries out these statements from top to bottom. Source code is a set of instructions; output is the result of carrying them out. Reading the last line first does not make that instruction run first.",
        "idea": "Follow one statement at a time. At the start the output is empty. After the first statement, it contains Open on its first line. After the second statement, Ready appears on the next line. The second statement adds output rather than replacing the first message. This example has no decisions or repetition, so each output statement runs once in the written order. The trace is a snapshot after each action, not a second program. Move backward in the trace to inspect an earlier snapshot; that control does not erase output in a real run.",
        "make": "Swap the messages so Ready appears before Open. You may exchange the two complete output statements or exchange their quoted text. Predict both lines before checking. The block arrangement exercise asks you to organize complete statements, which lets you concentrate on order without typing their punctuation. Its tests run the assembled program. When a result differs, compare the first output line before inspecting the second; one misplaced statement can explain both differences.",
        "recap": "In this straight-line example, statements run from top to bottom. Each output statement contributes its own line. Two correct messages can still form the wrong result when their order changes. Explain the difference between reading a program and running it: reading forms a prediction, while running executes the instructions. Cover the example and recall which message appears first, then use the trace to check your explanation. Later lessons introduce decisions and repetition; this lesson's order claim applies to the simple sequence shown here."
      },
      "fil": null,
      "ceb": null
    },
    "translationStatus": {
      "en": "reviewed",
      "fil": "draft",
      "ceb": "draft"
    }
  },
